//! The **HTTP/SSE and WebSocket binding**: connection identity and session routing.
//!
//! Pure. The rules here are transcribed from `agent-client-protocol-http`, because
//! none of them is derivable from the protocol itself — they are the transport's own
//! contract, and getting one wrong routes a message to the wrong reader rather than
//! failing loudly.
//!
//! # The shape
//!
//! ```text
//! POST   /acp   initialize → creates a connection, answers with Acp-Connection-Id
//! POST   /acp   later calls, carrying Acp-Connection-Id (+ Acp-Session-Id or params.sessionId)
//! GET    /acp   Accept: text/event-stream → SSE; one connection stream + one per session
//! GET    /acp   WebSocket upgrade → text frames, bidirectional
//! DELETE /acp   teardown
//! ```
//!
//! # The two rules that route everything
//!
//! 1. **Ten methods are session-scoped** and must name their session, in the header
//!    or in `params.sessionId`. An editor with two sessions open has two streams,
//!    and this is what decides which one an update reaches.
//! 2. **`$/`-prefixed messages are connection-scoped**, whatever a session header
//!    says. Cancellation is the reason: `$/cancel_request` refers to a request id on
//!    the CONNECTION, so routing it to a session stream would deliver it to a reader
//!    that has never heard of that id.
//!
//! # What is consumed today, stated plainly
//!
//! The header CONSTANTS and [`MAX_BODY_BYTES`] are live in both hosts. The
//! session-header routing — [`resolve_route`], [`apply_session_header`],
//! [`route_for_group`] — is the contract of the STREAMING transports (SSE and
//! WebSocket), which buzz does not serve yet: it is
//! POST-only and reads a session exclusively from `params.sessionId`, and its
//! route spec does not forward an `Acp-Session-Id` header. These rules stay here,
//! unit-proven, so a future streaming surface starts from the transport's own
//! contract instead of rediscovering it — but no host claim rests on them.

